 evidence as well as his summary  to William Barr he  not half but let's assume that he did  William bar summarized the summary not  the evidence and included just pieces of  four sentences from Robert molars  summary of his own investigation okay so  it's a summary of a summary rather than  a summary of the in full investigation  data why do we care about that the  reason we care is because Robert molars  summary is only about decisions made  either to prosecute or not to prosecute  without the further underlying evidence  so I'll give you an example to try to  make this crystal clear but Robert  molars summary of the investigation  could say based on all the evidence  about obstruction I decided I could not  prosecute Trump but I also cannot  exonerate him fine however if we looked  at the raw evidence from the  investigation it might indicate that  Robert Muller had for example 80 percent  of what he needed to prosecute Donald  Trump for obstruction Muller gives bar a  summary which says I didn't have enough  to indict on obstruction you might need  95 percent to indict on obstruction but  bar because he's summarizing a summary  says merely that there was not evidence  to indict for obstruction and implies  that there was not any evidence that  Donald Trump Trump obstructed justice  it's tricky wording so many people  simply don't notice the difference but  it's extraordinarily important the  strategic decision that William bar made  and he has a lot of experience in this  as an article from Tom Hartman tells us  which we'll deal with later on in the  program Barr made the strategic decision  to summarize the summary because it will  foment the idea of it's very binary was  there collusion or wasn't there  collusion is the president exonerated or  is the president not exonerated and if  we actually saw the full case file we  would almost certainly see a ton of  evidence supporting criminality by  Donald Trump and associates on many  fronts  but likely not enough 